  "title": "Largest Number",
  "summary": "Given a list of non-negative integers, arrange them to form the largest number possible and return the number as a string.",
  "statement": "Given a list of non-negative integers, arrange them to form the largest number possible and return the number as a string.",
  "constraints": [
    "1–100 nonnegative integers, each at most 10^9. Use every integer exactly once, keeping its digits together.",
    "Return the largest concatenation as a string. If all values are zero, return exactly '0', not repeated zeroes."
  ],
  "topics": [
    "arrays",
    "strings",
    "sorting",
    "greedy"
  ],
  "prerequisites": [],
  "signature": {
    "name": "largest_number",
    "parameters": [
      {
        "name": "nums",
        "annotation": "list[int]",
        "adapter": "raw",
        "source_parameter": null
      }
    ],
    "returns": "str",
    "return_adapter": "raw",
    "node_policy": "values",
    "result_mode": "return",
    "mutated_parameter": null
  },
  "starter_code": "def largest_number(nums: list[int]) -> str:\n    # Write your solution here.\n    pass\n",
  "reference_solution": "def largest_number(nums):\n    from functools import cmp_to_key\n    def compare(a, b):\n        ab, ba = a + b, b + a\n        return (ba > ab) - (ba < ab)\n    words = sorted(map(str, nums), key=cmp_to_key(compare))\n    if words[0] == \"0\":\n        return \"0\"\n    return \"\".join(words)\n",
  "brute_force_solution": null,
  "known_wrong_solutions": [
    "def largest_number(nums):\n    return \"\".join(map(str, sorted(nums, reverse=True))).lstrip(\"0\") or \"0\"\n",
    "def largest_number(nums):\n    return \"\".join(sorted(map(str, nums), reverse=True)).lstrip(\"0\") or \"0\"\n"
  ],
  "target_time_complexity": "O(n log n × d), d is the maximum digit count",
  "target_space_complexity": "O(n × d), including output and sorting storage",
  "estimated_minutes": 35,
  "rubric": {
    "algorithmic_insight": 2,
    "prerequisites": 1,
    "implementation": 1,
    "performance_pressure": 1,
    "edge_cases": 0
  },
  "hints": [
    "Choosing the largest number or the longest number first is not sufficient.",
    "For two strings a and b, compare a+b with b+a to decide their relative order.",
    "Sort using that pairwise concatenation comparator, join all strings, and normalize an all-zero result to '0'.",
    "Equal concatenations can use either relative order; compare the whole concatenation, not just first digits."
  ],
  "editorial": "Swapping an adjacent pair changes only a+b versus b+a, so placing the larger concatenation first cannot worsen the result. This comparison defines a consistent order (equivalently, compare each integer divided by 10^its_digit_count-1), allowing comparator-based sorting. The independent oracle enumerates every permutation of up to six values and selects the largest equal-length string; it does not reuse the sorting comparator. Output remains a string, including the 1000-digit stress case, so no large-number JSON precision is lost.",
